---
/*
 * The route loader's skeleton.
 *
 * A skeleton is a claim: "this is the shape of what is coming." The two versions
 * this replaces both broke the claim.
 *
 * The first was three 12px hairlines across the full shell at `top-20` — 372px
 * to the left of the article and directly over the running head, lining up with
 * no element on the page it was replacing. The second kept the same three bars
 * and sized them in `lh` of the title token, which turned them into 30px–43px
 * slabs. It did not make the navigation any slower; it made the loading state
 * impossible to miss, and a reader who watches a large grey shape arrive and
 * leave waits for it. Perceived speed is mostly this.
 *
 * So the skeleton is now a copy of the page's own opening, not a drawing of it.
 * Same shells, same tracks, same containers, same classes — with the text swapped
 * for bars. Not one row carries a hand-tuned height: each is `1lh` inside a
 * context that already declares the real font and leading, so it IS the real
 * element's line box, at any viewport, at any of the reader's text scales, and if
 * a type token changes tomorrow.
 *
 * Nor is anything positioned by a measured constant. The reading pages' chain —
 * `pt-4`, the running head, the mobile TOC row, the breadcrumb — is rebuilt out
 * of the page's own classes, so the skeleton lands on the breadcrumb because the
 * breadcrumb is there. The first version used a hand-measured `top: 9.25rem` and
 * a second `13.65rem` on mobile, and neither was right at a viewport between the
 * two they were measured at. The home hero is the one case whose top padding is
 * fluid, and it is applied as the same `py-title-block` class the hero uses.
 *
 * The hero is also the one page that is NOT in the reading surface: it spans the
 * full shell with its own two tracks and no rail. An earlier draft put its shape
 * inside the reading column like the other two, which put the display title
 * 372px to the right of where the real one lands — so the two branches here are
 * siblings under the frame, exactly as they are in `[...slug].astro`.
 *
 * Three shapes, because there are three page types and the destination is known
 * from the link before the fetch starts: `route-loader.mjs` maps `event.to` onto
 * `data-skeleton` at `astro:before-preparation`. Asking the incoming document is
 * not an option — at that event `event.newDocument` is the OUTGOING document, and
 * the fetched one only exists after the loader has resolved, which is after the
 * moment a loading state exists to cover.
 */

import BreadcrumbSkeleton from './BreadcrumbSkeleton.astro';
import LineBar from './LineBar.astro';
import ProseSkeleton from './ProseSkeleton.astro';
import { SKELETON_BAR as bar, SKELETON_BAR_INLINE as barInline } from '../lib/route-skeleton.mjs';
---

<style>
  /*
   * Three shapes, one loader, and exactly one of them on screen.
   *
   * `route-loader.mjs` knows the destination before the fetch starts and writes it
   * to one attribute on this element's root. Everything below follows from that one
   * string, which is why the shapes are all in the DOM: swapping `data-skeleton`
   * is the whole update, and a navigation costs one attribute write rather than a
   * walk over three subtrees.
   *
   * The hero is the awkward one, because it is not a variant of the reading
   * surface — it has no running head, no breadcrumb, no rail, and its own two
   * tracks. So it cannot simply be the third option: with `data-skeleton="home"`
   * the two reading branches go away entirely rather than being swapped out.
   */
  [data-skeleton-shape='notebook'],
  [data-skeleton-shape='section'],
  [data-skeleton-shape='home'] {
    display: none;
  }

  [data-skeleton='notebook'] [data-skeleton-shape='notebook'],
  [data-skeleton='section'] [data-skeleton-shape='section'],
  [data-skeleton='home'] [data-skeleton-shape='home'] {
    display: block;
  }

  [data-skeleton='home'] .route-skeleton-apparatus,
  [data-skeleton='home'] .route-skeleton-reading {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
   * `display: none` rather than `visibility: hidden` and rather than not rendering
   * the branch, because the two idle shapes are still in the document either way,
   * and this is the only difference a reader can see. It is also what the geometry
   * tests read: `checkVisibility()` on a shape whose ancestor is `display: none`
   * returns false, which is what lets `skeleton-mapping.spec.mjs` assert that
   * exactly one of the three is on screen instead of asserting the other two are
   * absent — a distinction that only matters if something later decides to keep
   * them in the DOM.
   */
</style>

{/*
  Hit testing is deliberately never re-enabled. The loader is a decorative
  `aria-hidden` skeleton plus an `sr-only` status message — nothing in it is
  interactive, so a visible-state variant that restores pointer events only makes
  a viewport-wide, z-50 click blocker sit over the content mid-navigation.

  That variant's class name is spelled out nowhere in this file, and that is not
  an accident. `src/**` is a declared Tailwind source and Tailwind's scanner does
  not skip comments, so writing the utility name here — even inside a comment
  explaining why it must not be used — emits a real rule at (0,2,0) specificity,
  which beats the base `pointer-events-none` at (0,1,0). The class was
  reintroduced exactly this way once and the loader silently became a click
  blocker again while every test stayed green.
  `test/integration/client-script-quality.test.mjs` compiles the sheet and asserts
  the utility is absent, so the next occurrence fails the build.
*/}
<div
  id="route-loader"
  data-route-loader
  data-skeleton="notebook"
  transition:persist="route-loader"
  role="status"
  aria-live="polite"
  aria-atomic="true"
  data-visible="false"
  class="pointer-events-none fixed inset-x-0 top-12 bottom-0 z-50 opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-[180ms] data-[visible=true]:opacity-100"
>
  <span data-route-loader-message class="sr-only"></span>

  <div aria-hidden="true" class="route-skeleton h-full bg-paper">
    <div class="route-skeleton-frame">
      {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- */}

      {/*
        The reading pages' own top chain, in a shell of its own — the page has two
        sibling `w-shell` wrappers, and collapsing them into one is what would put
        the running head 16px out from under the reading surface.
      */}
      <div class="route-skeleton-apparatus mx-auto w-shell pt-4">
        <div class="running-head flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
          {/*
            The bars are wrapped in bare spans, and that is the whole trick. The
            real running head is a flex row of two text spans, so its height is
            their line box — 11px of type on the body's 1.65 leading, 18.15px.
            Bars alone are 11px tall, which put every reading row 7px above where
            it belongs; a bar inside a span inherits the same font and leading, so
            the span is the same 18.15px the real one is and the row lands on the
            reading surface by itself.
          */}
          <span class="shrink-0">
            <span class={`${barInline} h-[0.6875rem] w-28`}></span>
          </span>
          <span class="min-w-0 shrink-0">
            <span class={`${barInline} h-[0.6875rem] w-36`}></span>
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/*
        `toc-rail-grid` is the page's own class, deliberately. An earlier draft
        carried a private `lg:grid-cols-[var(--width-toc-rail)_minmax(0,1fr)]`,
        which ignored `data-reading-toc` completely: with the rail shut the real
        article moves 120px left and the skeleton stayed put, 120px to the right
        of the text it was standing in for. Sharing the class means the reader's
        rail closes this grid too, on the same transition.

        `order-1`/`order-2` and the gap are the page's as well, which is what puts
        the mobile TOC stand-in above the breadcrumb on a phone and out of the way
        on a desktop.
      */}
      <div class="route-skeleton-reading toc-rail-grid mx-auto w-shell grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)] items-start gap-6 lg:grid-cols-[var(--width-toc-rail)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:gap-12">
        <div class="toc-rail-cell order-1 min-w-0">
          <div class="flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 lg:hidden">
            <span class={`${bar} h-[0.875rem] w-5 shrink-0`}></span>
            <span class={`${bar} h-[0.875rem] w-24`}></span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="order-2 flex min-w-0 justify-center">
          <div class="reading-column w-full min-w-0 max-w-reading">
            <div data-skeleton-shape="notebook">
              <BreadcrumbSkeleton />

              {/* The breadcrumb is a child of the READING COLUMN on the real page,
                 not of the article, so it is 70px wider than the prose. Capping
                 the shape at the measure narrowed the crumb row to 577px. */}
              <div class="max-w-measure">
                <div class="mb-8 border-b border-rule-strong pb-6" data-skeleton-row="header">
                  <div
                    class="m-0 font-serif text-title font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.025em]"
                    data-skeleton-row="title"
                  >
                    <LineBar width="80%" />
                  </div>
                  <div class="mt-3 mb-0 max-w-[55ch] font-sans text-ui leading-relaxed">
                    <LineBar width="100%" />
                    <LineBar width="75%" />
                  </div>
                  <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-[0.45rem] border-t border-rule pt-3 text-left">
                    <div class="running-head">
                      <span class={`${bar} h-[0.6875rem] w-20`}></span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="m-0 font-mono text-mono leading-[1.45]">
                      <LineBar width="18rem" />
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <ProseSkeleton paragraphs={[[100, 96, 62]]} />

                {/*
                The code frame is the one element whose height a skeleton cannot
                know: it is however many lines the block happens to have, which is
                a property of the page being loaded rather than of its type. So it
                is drawn at the right width and on the right ground — the measure,
                `--paper-raised`, a hairline, mono rows on the mono leading — and
                left to be roughly the right height. A number here would be the
                same mistake as the bars, wearing a different hat.
              */}
                <div
                  class="mt-prose-sibling overflow-hidden rounded-ui border border-rule bg-paper-raised p-4 font-mono text-mono leading-[1.5]"
                  data-skeleton-row="code"
                >
                  {Array.from({ length: 5 }, (_, index) => (
                    <LineBar width={`${[92, 74, 86, 58, 68][index]}%`} ratio={0.6} />
                  ))}
                </div>

                <ProseSkeleton paragraphs={[[100, 88], [72]]} />
              </div>
            </div>

            <div data-skeleton-shape="section">
              <BreadcrumbSkeleton />

              {/* The breadcrumb is a child of the READING COLUMN on the real page,
                 not of the article, so it is 70px wider than the prose. Capping
                 the shape at the measure narrowed the crumb row to 577px. */}
              <div class="max-w-measure">
                <div class="mb-8 border-b border-rule-strong pb-6" data-skeleton-row="header">
                  <div
                    class="m-0 font-serif text-title font-semibold leading-tight tracking-[-0.025em]"
                    data-skeleton-row="title"
                  >
                    <LineBar width="55%" />
                    <LineBar width="35%" />
                  </div>
                  <div class="mt-3 mb-0 max-w-[55ch] font-sans text-ui leading-relaxed">
                    <LineBar width="100%" />
                    <LineBar width="66%" />
                  </div>
                  <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-[0.45rem] border-t border-rule pt-3 text-left">
                    <div class="running-head">
                      <span class={`${bar} h-[0.6875rem] w-20`}></span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="m-0 font-mono text-mono leading-[1.45]">
                      <LineBar width="16rem" />
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                {/*
                A section index is its title and then the notebooks under it, so
                that is what this is: numbered rows, each a title line over a
                stand-in for its summary, separated by the list's own hairlines.
              */}
                <div class="m-0 list-none p-0" data-skeleton-row="list">
                  {[88, 96, 84, 100, 92].map((width, index) => (
                    <div class="border-t border-rule py-5 first:border-t-0 first:pt-0">
                      <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
                        <span class={`${bar} h-[0.6875rem] w-6 shrink-0`} />
                        <span class={`${bar} h-[1.375rem] w-[${width}%] shrink-0`} />
                      </div>
                      <span class={`${bar} mt-3 ml-9 h-[0.875rem] w-[${width - 18}%]`} />
                      {index === 0 && <span class={`${bar} mt-2 ml-9 h-[0.875rem] w-[56%]`} />}
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- */}

      {/*
        The hero, as its own top-level branch. `<section class="py-title-block">`
        with a `w-shell` inside it is literally `HomeTitleBlock.astro`'s outermost
        markup, including the rules above and below it, and the same two tracks:
        a fluid `1fr` and the fixed `--width-title-meta`. So the metadata margin
        starts at the x it will start at once the page lands, and the display
        title is capped at the measure exactly as the real one is.
      */}
      <div
        data-skeleton-shape="home"
        class="route-skeleton-home hero-composition border-y border-rule-strong py-title-block"
      >
        <div class="mx-auto w-shell">
          <div
            class="grid items-start gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_var(--width-title-meta)] lg:gap-16"
            data-skeleton-row="hero-grid"
          >
            <div class="min-w-0">
              <div
                class="mb-5 font-serif text-display font-bold leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.03em]"
                data-skeleton-row="title"
              >
                <LineBar width="100%" />
                <LineBar width="91%" />
                <LineBar width="80%" />
              </div>
              <div class="mb-8 max-w-[42ch] font-sans text-abstract leading-[1.6]">
                <LineBar width="100%" />
                <LineBar width="100%" />
                <LineBar width="60%" />
              </div>
              <span class="primary-control inline-flex items-center gap-2 leading-none">
                <span class={`${bar} h-[0.75rem] w-24`}></span>
              </span>
            </div>

            <div
              class="pt-2 lg:border-l lg:border-rule lg:pl-8 lg:pt-0 lg:text-right"
              data-skeleton-row="aside"
            >
              <span class={`${bar} ml-auto h-[0.875rem] w-40 max-w-full`}></span>
              <span class={`${bar} mt-2 ml-auto h-[0.875rem] w-28 max-w-full`}></span>
              <span class={`${bar} mt-5 ml-auto h-[1.5rem] w-full`}></span>
              <span class={`${bar} mt-2 ml-auto h-[0.875rem] w-full`}></span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- */}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
